WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,236
Indicators of compromise, or IOCs, are observable details that may be associated with suspicious or malicious activity.

2
00:00:08,236 --> 00:00:20,070
Common examples include IP addresses, domains, URLs, file hashes, email artifacts, registry paths, process names, certificate details, and malware configuration values.

3
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:29,346
Indicators are useful because they can enrich alerts, support searches, help scope incidents, and sometimes drive temporary blocking.

4
00:00:29,346 --> 00:00:33,903
They are often the first CTI artifacts that new analysts encounter.

5
00:00:33,903 --> 00:00:35,903
Indicators also have limits.

6
00:00:35,903 --> 00:00:41,260
Many are brittle, meaning they can change quickly or appear in benign contexts.

7
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:44,218
An IP address can be shared infrastructure.

8
00:00:44,218 --> 00:00:46,617
A domain can expire or be sinkholed.

9
00:00:46,617 --> 00:00:50,455
A file hash changes when the file changes by even one byte.

10
00:00:50,455 --> 00:00:54,134
A process name can be copied from legitimate software.

11
00:00:54,134 --> 00:00:59,331
A registry path may be meaningful in one intrusion but irrelevant in another.

12
00:00:59,331 --> 00:01:06,608
Using indicators without context can create false positives, missed detections, and unnecessary blocks.

13
00:01:06,608 --> 00:01:13,164
Tactics, techniques, and procedures, usually called TTPs, describe behavior at a higher level.

14
00:01:13,164 --> 00:01:27,237
A tactic is the adversary's objective, such as initial access, persistence, privilege escalation, defense evasion, credential access, discovery, lateral movement, collection, exfiltration, or impact.

15
00:01:27,237 --> 00:01:31,156
A technique describes a way to accomplish that objective.

16
00:01:31,156 --> 00:01:37,153
Procedures are the specific ways a threat actor or campaign has implemented a technique.

17
00:01:37,153 --> 00:01:44,989
TTPs are often more durable than single indicators because behavior can persist even when infrastructure changes.

18
00:01:44,989 --> 00:01:51,386
MITRE ATT&CK is a widely used knowledge base for organizing adversary tactics and techniques.

19
00:01:51,386 --> 00:01:59,062
It gives defenders a shared language for mapping observations, detections, hunt hypotheses, and coverage gaps.

20
00:01:59,062 --> 00:02:09,057
ATT&CK is not a complete model of every threat and it should not be used mechanically, but it is useful for connecting low-level evidence to behavior.

21
00:02:09,057 --> 00:02:20,172
For example, an unusual process, command-line pattern, and authentication event may be more meaningful when mapped to a likely technique and defensive objective.

22
00:02:20,172 --> 00:02:23,050
The distinction matters during response.

23
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:30,087
If a team only blocks a list of domains, the adversary or campaign may shift infrastructure and continue.

24
00:02:30,087 --> 00:02:46,479
If the team also understands the behavior, such as suspicious authentication patterns, unusual script execution, or unexpected data staging, defenders can build detections and hunts that remain useful after individual indicators expire.

25
00:02:46,479 --> 00:02:48,718
Indicators are often fast to use.

26
00:02:48,718 --> 00:02:51,037
Behavior is often more resilient.

27
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:56,074
A mature CTI workflow connects indicators to behavior whenever possible.

28
00:02:56,074 --> 00:03:06,389
Instead of treating every IOC as equally meaningful, ask what the indicator represents, how it was observed, how recent it is, what behavior it supports,

29
00:03:06,389 --> 00:03:09,348
and whether that behavior matters locally.

30
00:03:09,348 --> 00:03:12,386
Indicators can be tactical starting points.

31
00:03:12,386 --> 00:03:19,823
TTPs help defenders build more resilient detections, better hunts, and clearer incident response guidance.

32
00:03:19,823 --> 00:03:23,021
This does not mean indicators are unimportant.

33
00:03:23,021 --> 00:03:28,618
They are often useful for quick scoping, enrichment, and short-term containment.

34
00:03:28,618 --> 00:03:30,857
The key is to label their purpose.

35
00:03:30,857 --> 00:03:38,294
An indicator used for enrichment may be acceptable with lower confidence than an indicator used for blocking.

36
00:03:38,294 --> 00:03:42,771
Matching the control to the confidence reduces unnecessary harm.
